stood in the line of speakers with his poem about the parking ticket
and was asked, very kindly, whether he could limit it to three minutes.
He did four. The block roared. For a moment the white carpet looked
like a floor where people lived.
   Then the seating changed.
   It happened quietly, the way these things do. Ushers moved among
the audience with polite hands. People from the old pilot, I recognized
them now by their posture, were guided toward the ring of chairs
around the central platform. One by one, they took their seats, hands in
their laps, eyes on the floor. Forty of them. Thirty-nine adults from the
old pilot, and a boy of maybe twelve I didn’t know, from the new one.
   And in the ring, in the chair nearest the lectern, a very still man in a
gray jacket sat down, one shoulder carried high.
   “Mikey,” I said, to myself.
   He kept his eyes on the floor like the others and sat as if holding a
bowl full of water.
   Behind me, the main doors opened.
   A small woman in a church dress came in alone, carrying her purse
in both hands and nothing else. She stopped inside the door and
looked at the vastness of the hall, the carpet and the steel and the
champagne, like a woman who’d been told to walk across a river on
stones.
   I’d never seen Doña Luz look afraid.
   She went to the front. Nobody stopped her. An usher offered her a
seat in the front row, because that’s where Aperture seats the mothers,
and she took it, and folded her hands. She was ten feet from the plat‐
form. She didn’t look at the champagne or the donors. She looked at
her son.
   Mikey’s head came up.
   For one second, his face did something I can’t describe, an animal’s
look, caught in the open. His mouth moved. I don’t know what it said.
He looked down again at his hands.
   I saw the hum stop. I mean I felt it, through the contact mic, through
the floor. A gap, the length of a breath, where a low note should have
been.



